'''Yinghuo-1''' ({{zh|s=萤火一号|t=螢火一號|p=Yínghuǒ yī hào}}) was a Chinese [[Exploration of Mars|Mars-exploration]] [[space probe]], intended to be the first Chinese planetary space probe and the first Chinese spacecraft to orbit Mars. It was launched from [[Baikonur Cosmodrome]], Kazakhstan, on 8 November 2011, along with the [[Russia]]n [[Fobos-Grunt]] [[sample return]] spacecraft, which was intended to visit Mars's moon [[Phobos (moon)|Phobos]].<ref name=NatureNov42011/><ref>[https://www.universetoday.com/89845/daring-russian-sample-return-mission-to-martian-moon-phobos-aims-for-november-liftoff/ "Daring Russian sample return mission to Martian moon Phobos aims for November Liftoff"]. Universe Today, 2011-10-13. Retrieved 17 October 2011.</ref> The 115-kg (250-lb) Yinghuo-1 probe was intended by the [[CNSA]] to orbit Mars for about two years,<ref name="planetary" /> studying the planet's surface, atmosphere, [[ionosphere]] and [[magnetic field]].{{citation needed|date=December 2020}} Shortly after launch, Fobos-Grunt was expected to perform two burns to depart Earth orbit bound for Mars. However, these burns did not take place, leaving both probes stranded in orbit.<ref name="failed burn">{{cite web|url=http://ria.ru/science/20111109/484401134.html|script-title=ru:Маршевая двигательная установка станции "Фобос-Грунт" не сработала|publisher=[[RIA Novosti]]|date=9 November 2011|access-date=20 November 2011|language=ru}}</ref> On 17 November 2011, CNSA reported that Yinghuo-1 had been declared lost.<ref name=DeclaredLost>[http://www.spacedaily.com/reports/Yinghuo_Was_Worth_It_999.html "Yinghuo Was Worth It"]. Space Daily, Morris Jones, 17 November 2011. Retrieved 19 November 2011.</ref> After a period of [[orbital decay]], Yinghuo-1 and Fobos-Grunt underwent destructive re-entry on 15 January 2012, finally disintegrating over the Pacific Ocean.<ref name=ABCFinished>[https://abcnews.go.com/Technology/phobos-grunt-failed-russian-mars-probe-falls-earth/story?id=15366151 "Phobos-Grunt: Failed Russian Mars Probe Falls to Earth"]. ABC News, 15 January 2012.</ref><ref name=BBCJan15>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/science-environment-16491457 "Phobos-Grunt: Failed probe likely to return late Sunday"]. [[BBC News]], 15 January 2012.</ref>

As a result, CNSA subsequently moved to embark on an independent Mars exploration program, which culminated in the [[Tianwen-1]] orbiter-lander-rover mission that successfully landed the [[Zhurong (rover)|Zhurong]] rover on Mars on 22 May, 2021.<ref name="NYT-20210514">{{cite news |last1=Myers |first1=Steven Lee |last2=Chang |first2=Kenneth |date=14 May 2021 |title=China's Mars Rover Mission Lands on the Red Planet |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/14/science/china-mars.html |url-access=limited |access-date=16 May 2021}}</ref>

==Name==
Yinghuo-1's name ({{zh|s=萤火|t=螢火|p=yínghuǒ|tr=[[firefly]]|l=luminous fire}}) was a tribute to the near-[[homophone]] {{zh|s=荧惑|t=熒惑|p=yínghuò|out=p|labels=no}}. This word, a short form of "shimmering planet" ({{zh|t=熒惑星|p=yínghuò xīng|labels=no}}), is an ancient Chinese name for [[Mars]].

==Background==
On 26 March 2007, the director of the [[China National Space Administration]], [[Sun Laiyan]], and the head of the [[Russian Space Agency]], [[Anatoly Perminov]], signed a landmark space co-operation accord, entitled the "Cooperative Agreement between the China National Space Administration and the Russian Space Agency on joint Chinese-Russian exploration of Mars". One stipulation of the agreement was the construction and launch of the Yinghuo Mars orbiter and its Russian counterpart, Fobos-Grunt.<ref name="nasa" />

==Instruments and objectives==
Yinghuo-1's primary scientific objectives were:
# To conduct detailed investigation of the plasma environment and [[magnetic field]] around Mars.
# To study Martian ion escape processes and their possible mechanisms
# To conduct ionosphere [[occultation]] measurements between Yinghuo-1 and Fobos-Grunt, focusing on the sub-solar and midnight regions.
# To observe [[Dust storm|sandstorms]] on the Martian surface.

The probe's science payload consisted of four instrument:<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://english.peopledaily.com.cn/200705/30/eng20070530_379330.html|access-date=12 December 2009|title=China and Russia join hands to explore Mars|publisher=[[People's Daily Online]]|date=30 May 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.agu.org/meetings/chapman/2008/acall/Prelim-Program.pdf|access-date=12 December 2009|title=Chapman Conference on the Solar Wind Interaction with Mars|date=12 November 2007|location=San Diego, California|archive-date=6 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110606040915/http://www.agu.org/meetings/chapman/2008/acall/Prelim-Program.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

# A [[plasma (physics)|plasma]] package, consisting of an [[Electrostatic analyzer|electron analyzer]], [[ion]] analyzer and [[mass spectrometer]].
# A [[fluxgate magnetometer]].
# A radio-occultation sounder.
# An optical imaging system consisting of two cameras with {{convert|200|m|ft|abbr=on}} resolution for high-quality images of the Martian surface to be captured from orbit.

==Mission profile==
[[File:Orbit Diagram of Yinghuo-1 and Fobos-Grunt.svg|thumb|Diagram showing Yinghuo-1's intended approach pathway to Mars.]]
Following its transit to Mars, Yinghuo-1 was planned to separate from Fobos-Grunt in October 2012<ref name="russianspace" /> and enter a 72.8-hour equatorial Martian orbit,<ref name="planetary" /> with an [[orbital inclination]] of approximately 5 [[degree (angle)|degrees]].{{citation needed|date=May 2011}} Fobos-Grunt and Yinghuo-1 would have conducted Mars [[ionosphere]] [[occultation]] experiments together,<ref name=QikanWen>{{cite web|url=http://www.cjss.ac.cn/qikan/manage/wenzhang/2008-05-06.pdf|title=YingHuo-1 – Martian Space Environment Exploration Orbiter|access-date=20 October 2011|archive-date=31 March 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120331032256/http://www.cjss.ac.cn/qikan/manage/wenzhang/2008-05-06.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> although Fobos-Grunt's primary objective was to obtain [[sample return|surface samples]] from the Martian moon [[Phobos (moon)|Phobos]]. Yinghuo-1 would have experienced periods of up to 8.8 hours in darkness when its orbit carried it over Mars's nightside; it would have run on battery power during these periods, as its [[solar panel]]s would have been unusable without direct sunlight.<ref name="planetary" />

===Launch processing===
On 17 October 2011, the completed Yinghuo-1 satellite arrived at Baikonur Cosmodrome with Fobos-Grunt, beginning payload processing operations in preparation for its November launch.<ref>[https://www.universetoday.com/90012/phobos-grunt-and-yinghou-1-arrive-at-baikonur-launch-site-to-tight-mars-deadline/ "Phobos-Grunt and Yinghuo-1 arrive at Baikonur Launch Site"]. Universe Today, 19 October 2011. Retrieved 20 October 2011.</ref>

===Launch and orbital burn failure===
China's Yinghuo-1 and the Russian Fobos-Grunt spacecraft were launched together aboard a [[Ukraine|Ukrainian]] [[Zenit rocket]] with a [[Fregat]] upper stage from [[Baikonur Cosmodrome]], Kazakhstan, on 8 November 2011.<ref name=NatureNov42011/><ref name="russianspace">{{cite web | url=https://www.russianspaceweb.com/phobos_grunt.html | title=Phobos-Grunt mission | publisher=Russianspaceweb.com | date=7 May 2011 | access-date=30 May 2011 | author=Zak, Anatoly}}</ref> Shortly after launch, Fobos-Grunt was expected to perform two burns to depart Earth orbit and begin its journey to Mars. However, these burns did not take place, stranding the two spacecraft in their parking orbit.<ref name="failed burn-2">{{cite web|url=http://ria.ru/science/20111109/484401134.html|script-title=ru:Маршевая двигательная установка станции "Фобос-Грунт" не сработала|publisher=RIA Novosti|date=9 November 2011|access-date=9 November 2011|language=ru}}</ref> Despite repeated efforts to contact the launcher and rectify the problem, the spacecraft continued to lose altitude.<ref>[https://www.npr.org/blogs/thetwo-way/2011/11/11/142241163/russia-running-out-of-time-as-mars-mission-seems-destined-to-fail "Russia Running Out of Time, As Mars Mission Seems Destined To Fail"]. [[NPR]], 11 November 2011. Retrieved 14 November 2011.</ref> On 17 November, Chinese state media formally declared the Yinghuo-1 probe lost,<ref name=DeclaredLost/> and the launcher's orbit gradually began to decay.

===Destructive re-entry===
On 14 January 2012, it was reported that Fobos-Grunt and Yinghuo-1 were beginning their final descent into Earth's atmosphere, falling at a rate of several hundred metres per hour from their {{convert|147|km|mi|adj=on}} orbital altitude.<ref name=BBCJan15/> The two spacecraft completed their re-entry and disintegrated over the Pacific Ocean on 15 January 2012.<ref name=ABCFinished/>

==Specifications==
*Length: {{convert|0.75|m|ft}} <small>(excluding solar panels)</small>.<ref name=HighXin>[http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2007-05/22/content_6132750.htm "China to launch first Mars probe in 2009"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120306154929/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2007-05/22/content_6132750.htm |date=6 March 2012 }}. Xinhua, 22 May 2007. Retrieved 19 November 2011.</ref>
*Width: {{convert|0.75|m|ft}}.<ref name=HighXin/>
*Height: {{convert|0.6|m|ft}}.<ref name=HighXin/>
*Mass: {{convert|115|kg|lb}}.<ref name=QikanWen/>
*Power: 3-axis stabilised, 2×3 section [[Photovoltaic module|solar array]] with a full-extended length of {{convert|5.6|m|ft}}, providing average power of 90 W, and peak power of 180 W.<ref name=QikanWen/>
*[[High-gain antenna|HGA]]: 950&nbsp;mm antenna dish ([[S-band]]) with a 12 W transmitter in two frequencies (8.4 and 7.17&nbsp;GHz) and a data rate between 8 bit/s and 16&nbsp;kbit/s.<ref name=QikanWen/>
*[[Low-gain antenna|LGA]]: 80 bit/s data rate.<ref name=QikanWen/>

